## Procedure Form Template: Template #01

The root file `Template # 01.docx` is a reusable Quality System Procedure template. It should become a structured form and document template in the application rather than remaining only as a Word file.

### Extracted Header Fields

The template contains an approval and ownership block for:

- Prepared By
- Reviewed By
- Approved By
- Revision Date
- Process Owner

The application should store these as structured fields linked to tenant users and the document approval workflow. They should not be treated as unstructured text only.

### Extracted Procedure Sections

The main procedure template contains these editable sections:

1. Purpose
2. Scope
3. Responsibilities
4. Procedure
5. Records
6. Related Documents
7. Compliance Note

The Procedure section is the main operational content. The Records section should link to the applicable Level 4 blank forms, formats, or logbooks. When those templates are completed during execution, the resulting entries become quality records.

### Extracted Supporting Tables

The template includes:

- Revision History: Page Number, Revision Number, Change Description
- Records Register: Form Number and Title
- Related Documents Register: Document Number and Title
- Compliance Register: Standard, edition, and clause reference

The compliance examples in the template include API Spec Q2 2nd Edition, ISO 9001:2015, ISO 14001:2015, and ISO 45001:2018. These should be configurable because each tenant may use different standards.

### Document Header and Controlled Identity

The Word header contains placeholders for:

- Title
- Document or procedure number
- Revision number
- Page X of Y

The application should generate these values consistently for previews, PDF exports, and approved copies.

### Recommended Form Architecture

The procedure form should be composed of:

```text
Document Metadata
  |
  +-- Approval and Ownership
  +-- Revision History
  +-- Rich Procedure Content
  +-- Related Level 4 Templates
  +-- Related Documents
  +-- Compliance References
```

The first implementation should support saving a draft, assigning the process owner, routing the procedure through Prepared By / Reviewed By / Approved By, recording revisions, and publishing an approved version. The Word template can remain an import/export reference while the structured form becomes the system's authoritative working record.

### Level 4 Forms and Quality Records Implementation

The Level 4 form workspace is available at:

```text
/forms
/forms/templates/new
/forms/:id
```

The secured backend API supports:

```text
GET  /forms/templates
GET  /forms/templates/:id
POST /forms/templates
GET  /forms/records
POST /forms/records
```

`FormTemplate` and `FormField` represent the blank controlled form. `QualityRecord` represents data captured after a person performs the activity. Required fields are validated before a completed record is stored. The record is tenant-scoped and linked to the user who completed it.

Level 4 templates can optionally link to a parent procedure or work instruction. `FILE` fields accept PDF, JPEG, and PNG uploads after a quality record is created:

```text
POST /files/records/:recordId
```

The file is stored separately from the completed record, with tenant ownership, checksum, MIME type, and uploader metadata preserved.

### Procedure Form

The first structured procedure form is available at:

```text
http://localhost:3000/documents/procedures/new
```

The current UI includes:

- document title, control number, issue/revision, review interval, department, and effective date
- Prepared By, Reviewed By, and Approved By assignment cards
- organizational designation selection for the approval chain
- Purpose, Scope, Responsibilities, and Procedure fields
- Records and related-document fields
- applicable standard and clause reference
- revision history row
- Save Draft and Submit for Review actions

The form now sends structured procedure data to NestJS using the authenticated JWT. Save Draft creates a persisted draft, while Submit for Review creates the document and moves it to `PENDING_APPROVAL`. Effective dates, review intervals, compliance references, and revision history are persisted; parent Level 4 template selection and richer revision-history editing remain UI enhancements.

### CEO/MD Approver Workspace

The approval workspace is available at:

```text
http://localhost:3000/documents/approvals
```

It represents the experience for the person assigned as **Approved By**, normally the CEO or Managing Director. The workspace includes:

- documents awaiting the approver's decision
- document control number, issue/revision, department, and due date
- Process Owner, Prepared By, and Reviewed By identities
- purpose and procedure summary
- compliance standard and clause
- related Level 4 forms and related documents
- revision context
- approval comments
- Approve and Sign action
- Return for Correction action

The approver does not silently edit the document. Approval applies to the selected document version and records the decision. Returning a document sends it back to the process owner for correction while preserving the approver's comments in the future audit trail.

The workspace now loads assigned documents from `GET /documents/approvals` and records decisions through `POST /documents/versions/:versionId/decision`. Approval comments and document-version decisions are persisted by the backend. Electronic signature, notifications, and a richer document preview remain future additions.

### Change Request Workflow

The first slice of Process 2 is available at:

```text
/documents/change-requests
/documents/change-requests/new
```

The secured API supports:

```text
POST /documents/change-requests
GET  /documents/change-requests
GET  /documents/change-requests/:id
POST /documents/change-requests/:id/review
```

The workflow captures the approved document, existing requirement, proposed change, and reason for change. A tenant user submits the request as `SUBMITTED`. A General Manager or Management Representative in the same tenant can accept it as `ACCEPTED_FOR_CHANGE` or reject it, with reviewer identity, comment, and timestamp persisted.

Accepted requests now create the next draft version, supersede the prior version, copy the original Prepared By / Reviewed By / Approved By assignments, and link the new version back to the request. The new revision then completes the normal review, approval, and publication cycle.

The publication endpoint is:

```text
POST /documents/versions/:versionId/publish
```

Only an approved version can be published. Publishing marks the selected version `CURRENT`, marks older versions `SUPERSEDED`, and changes the parent document to `PUBLISHED`.

### Separate Login Experiences

The application has separate entry points for tenant users and the Platform Admin:

```text
/login                   Tenant user login
/login/platform-admin    Platform Admin login
```

The tenant login asks for:

- company subdomain
- user email
- password

The Platform Admin login asks for:

- platform email
- password

This separation reflects the security boundary in the product. Tenant users enter one company workspace, while the Platform Admin can manage the platform and all client tenants.

The backend authentication endpoints are:

```text
POST /auth/tenant/login
POST /auth/platform/login
```

Successful requests return a signed JWT with a distinct scope: `TENANT_USER` or `PLATFORM_ADMIN`. Local development accounts can be created with:

```powershell
cd 'C:/Users/User/ERP/backend'
$env:DATABASE_URL='postgresql://postgres:Haroon@123@localhost:5432/erp'
$env:SEED_PASSWORD='ChangeMe123!'
npm run db:seed
```

Seeded local accounts:

- Platform Admin: `hamzakhannaghar1998@gmail.com`
- Tenant user / Tenant Admin: `tenant.admin@acme.com`, subdomain `acme`
- Tenant CEO/MD approver: `ceo@acme.com`, subdomain `acme`

The seeded password is for local development only. Production work still requires MFA, session management, secure secrets, refresh-token/revocation handling, and broader authorization coverage.

### Protected API Access

The backend now verifies Bearer JWTs on protected routes:

- Tenant Admin member routes require a valid `TENANT_USER` token with `isTenantAdmin: true` and a tenant ID.
- Document and approval routes require a valid JWT.
- Approval queue and decision identity are derived from the authenticated user rather than a request-supplied email.
- Tenant Admin member routes derive the tenant ID from the authenticated JWT rather than using a hardcoded tenant.

Smoke-test result:

```text
Unauthenticated /tenant-admin/members: 401
Authenticated Tenant Admin /tenant-admin/members: 200
```

The remaining security work is to extend Platform Admin authorization to platform APIs, add role-specific document authorization, and implement MFA, session management, and token revocation.

### Document and Approval API

The first Prisma-backed document endpoints are available through the NestJS backend:

```text
POST /documents/procedures
GET  /documents/:id
POST /documents/:id/submit
GET  /documents/approvals?approverEmail=user@example.com
POST /documents/versions/:versionId/decision
```

The API currently supports:

- creating a Level 2 procedure with a first version
- assigning Prepared By, Reviewed By, and Approved By users by email
- loading a document with versions, assignments, compliance references, and revision history
- submitting draft or returned documents for approval
- loading the approval queue for an assigned approver
- recording an approval or return-for-correction decision

The database must contain the referenced tenant and users before a procedure can be created. Authentication, authorization, tenant-context extraction, email invitations, and production request validation are still required before these endpoints are exposed to real users.

