## Local Installation Steps

Follow the steps below to set up the local development environment for the ERP project.

---

## Platform Admin Page

The Platform Admin is the product owner and manages all client tenants from a platform-level console. The initial prototype uses the hardcoded identity:

```text
hamzakhannaghar1998@gmail.com
```

The page is available at:

```text
http://localhost:3001/platform-admin
```

### Platform Admin capabilities

- View all client companies in the platform
- Review tenant subdomains, status, user counts, and recent activity
- Search and filter tenants
- Assign or replace a Tenant Admin using an email address
- Suspend or activate a tenant in the prototype UI
- Review platform activity and access boundaries

The first prototype includes five client tenants:

- Acme Manufacturing
- Northstar Healthcare
- Meridian Logistics
- Vertex Technologies
- BluePeak Consulting

### Role boundary

- Platform Admin: manages all tenants and platform-level operations
- Tenant Admin: manages users, roles, and permissions inside one company only

The current Platform Admin page uses in-memory prototype data. Tenant records, admin assignments, status changes, and platform audit logs still need NestJS, Prisma, and PostgreSQL APIs. Platform Admin authentication already has a separate JWT login endpoint.

### 1. Initialize the project directory

Create the workspace folder for the ERP application and initialize the Node.js project:

```bash
npm init -y
```

### 2. Set up the NestJS backend

Scaffold the backend using the NestJS CLI:

```bash
npx @nestjs/cli new backend --skip-git --package-manager npm --strict
```

This creates the backend application structure for the API layer that will eventually connect to Prisma and PostgreSQL.

### 3. Installed Prisma ORM

Installed Prisma as a development dependency:

```bash
npm install prisma --save-dev
```

This adds Prisma to the project so schema management and migration tooling are available during development.

### 4. Installed Prisma Client and PostgreSQL Dependencies

Installed the Prisma Client, PostgreSQL adapter, and PostgreSQL Node.js driver:

```bash
npm install @prisma/client @prisma/adapter-pg pg
```

These packages provide the database client layer and PostgreSQL connectivity needed for application data access.

### 5. Approved Prisma Installation Scripts

npm initially blocked Prisma package installation scripts as a security precaution.

Approved the required Prisma packages:

```bash
npm install-scripts approve prisma @prisma/engines
```

This allows the Prisma engine binaries and related installation scripts to run correctly in the local environment.

